<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#arabic-language">Glossary</a> · Term · language</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arabic language</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Arabic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arabic is a Semitic language that spread with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-peninsula">Arabian Peninsula</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> it became the language of administration, replacing Greek and Persian in government records. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> it became a major language of science, philosophy and literature, and it heavily influenced Persian after the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arabic-language.jpg" alt="Arabic language" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aieman Khimji · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Learning_Arabic_calligraphy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Arabic is classified as a Central Semitic language of the Afroasiatic family. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> had a wide variety of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semitic-languages">Semitic languages</a> in antiquity, among them Ancient South Arabian languages in the southwest, Dadanitic and Taymanitic in the oases of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> and Hasaitic in the east. Safaitic and Hismaic, attested on the northwestern frontier, share important features with later forms of Arabic, and scholars theorise that they are early forms of it, to be counted as Old Arabic. Linguists generally believe that Old Arabic, a collection of related dialects, first emerged during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>.</p><p>Classical Arabic, the modern Arabic varieties and the Safaitic and Hismaic inscriptions share features found in no other Central Semitic language, evidence of descent from a hypothetical ancestor, Proto-Arabic. Because several Arabic varieties keep features that Classical Arabic lacks, the vernaculars cannot descend from Classical Arabic, which is a sister language rather than their direct ancestor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Arabic usually means Standard Arabic, which Western linguists divide into Classical Arabic and Modern Standard Arabic. Classical Arabic is the language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>, used from Pre-Islamic Arabia to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, and follows the norms set by classical grammarians such as Sibawayh and the vocabulary of classical dictionaries such as the Lisān al-ʻArab. Modern Standard Arabic largely follows its grammar and vocabulary but has taken new vocabulary for concepts of the industrial and post-industrial era. Alongside it stand regional vernacular dialects, usually acquired in families, which are not necessarily mutually intelligible. Educated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> speak both, a situation called diglossia.</p><p>Arabic has a root-and-pattern morphology. A root of usually three bare consonants is fitted into a pattern to form words: from k-t-b, meaning write, come katabtu, I wrote, kitābun, book, and kātibun, writer. It is written with the Arabic alphabet, an abjad that runs from right to left and derives from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> through Nabatean.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>During the Middle Ages Arabic was a major vehicle of culture and learning, especially in science, mathematics and philosophy, and many European languages borrowed words from it. The influence is seen mainly in Spanish, and less in Portuguese, Catalan and Sicilian, owing to the long Arabic cultural and linguistic presence in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">Al-Andalus</a> era. Maltese is a Semitic language that developed from a dialect of Arabic and is written in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet.</p><p>Persian, Turkish, Hindustani, Malay, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> are among the languages most markedly influenced. The lexicographic tradition lasted about a millennium before the modern period: the Kitāb al-ʿAyn of the 8th century is considered the first lexicon to include all Arabic roots, and Ibn Manzur's Lisān al-ʿArab of 1290 gives 9,273 roots. Arabic is one of the official languages of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> and the liturgical language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arabic” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
